<section class="lesson-card" id="quality-charts">
<h2>6. Why c and u charts are both Poisson</h2>
<p>Both <strong>c charts</strong> and <strong>u charts</strong> are used for <strong>defect counts</strong>. A defect is a random event, and more than one defect can occur on the same unit. That is why both charts are based on the <strong>Poisson distribution</strong>.</p>
<div class="big-memory">The only question is whether every observation had the same opportunity for defects to occur.</div>
<div class="grid-2">
<div class="concept-card">
<h3>c chart: equal opportunity</h3>
<p>Use a c chart when every inspected unit is the same size and is inspected in the same way.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Every steel plate is 2 m × 2 m, and the full surface of every plate is inspected.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Plate</th><th>Defects</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>3</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Because every plate gives the same inspection opportunity, the raw defect counts can be compared directly.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept-card">
<h3>u chart: unequal opportunity</h3>
<p>Use a u chart when the inspected sizes, lengths, areas, or sample quantities are different.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Plates have different surface areas.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Plate</th><th>Area, m²</th><th>Defects</th><th>Defects/m²</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>A</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>1.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>B</td><td>6</td><td>5</td><td>0.83</td></tr>
<tr><td>C</td><td>10</td><td>7</td><td>0.70</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Plate C has the largest raw count, but it has the lowest defect rate. A u chart corrects for the unequal opportunity.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="callout good">
<strong>Why both are Poisson:</strong> In both cases, the underlying random variable is the number of defect events. The u chart does not change the probability model; it divides the Poisson count by the amount of inspection opportunity.
</div>
<h3>A comparison that makes it stick</h3>
<p>Imagine students taking exams.</p>
<div class="grid-2">
<div class="concept-card">
<h3>Same one-page exam</h3>
<p>If every student answers the same number of questions, compare the raw number of mistakes.</p>
<p><strong>This is like a c chart.</strong></p>
</div>
<div class="concept-card">
<h3>Different-length exams</h3>
<p>If one student answers 20 questions and another answers 100, compare mistakes per question.</p>
<p><strong>This is like a u chart.</strong></p>
</div>
</div>
<h3>Decision rule</h3>
<div class="formula" style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:1rem;text-align:left;white-space:pre-wrap">
Are you counting defects?
        │
        ▼
      Yes
   (Poisson)
        │
        ▼
Is the inspection opportunity equal?
        │
   ┌────┴────┐
   │         │
  Yes        No
   │         │
c chart    u chart
(raw count) (defects per unit)
</div>
<div class="big-memory"><strong>Memory trick:</strong> c = count. u = defects per unit.</div>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Chart or model</th><th>What it monitors</th><th>Underlying distribution</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>np chart</td><td>Number of defective units when sample size is constant</td><td>Binomial</td></tr>
<tr><td>p chart</td><td>Proportion defective when sample size varies</td><td>Binomial</td></tr>
<tr><td>c chart</td><td>Defect count when opportunity is constant</td><td>Poisson</td></tr>
<tr><td>u chart</td><td>Defects per unit when opportunity varies</td><td>Poisson</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exponential model</td><td>Time, distance, or area until the next event</td><td>Exponential</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>
<!-- EDIT: c and u chart opportunity lab -->
<section class="lesson-card" id="attribute-lab">
<h2>10. Interactive c/u chart opportunity lab</h2>
<p class="section-intro">Enter defect counts and inspection opportunities. The lab automatically selects a c chart when opportunity is constant and a u chart when opportunity varies. It then calculates the centre line and basic 3σ limits.</p>
<div class="interactive-shell">
<div class="editable-table">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Sample</th><th>Defects cᵢ</th><th>Opportunity nᵢ</th><th>Rate uᵢ = cᵢ/nᵢ</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>1</td><td><label class="sr-only" for="attrDefects1">Sample 1 defect count</label><input id="attrDefects1" min="0" step="1" type="number" value="4"/></td><td><label class="sr-only" for="attrOpportunity1">Sample 1 inspection opportunity</label><input id="attrOpportunity1" min="0.001" step="0.5" type="number" value="2"/></td><td id="attrRate1">—</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><label class="sr-only" for="attrDefects2">Sample 2 defect count</label><input id="attrDefects2" min="0" step="1" type="number" value="5"/></td><td><label class="sr-only" for="attrOpportunity2">Sample 2 inspection opportunity</label><input id="attrOpportunity2" min="0.001" step="0.5" type="number" value="2"/></td><td id="attrRate2">—</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><label class="sr-only" for="attrDefects3">Sample 3 defect count</label><input id="attrDefects3" min="0" step="1" type="number" value="3"/></td><td><label class="sr-only" for="attrOpportunity3">Sample 3 inspection opportunity</label><input id="attrOpportunity3" min="0.001" step="0.5" type="number" value="2"/></td><td id="attrRate3">—</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><label class="sr-only" for="attrDefects4">Sample 4 defect count</label><input id="attrDefects4" min="0" step="1" type="number" value="6"/></td><td><label class="sr-only" for="attrOpportunity4">Sample 4 inspection opportunity</label><input id="attrOpportunity4" min="0.001" step="0.5" type="number" value="2"/></td><td id="attrRate4">—</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><label class="sr-only" for="attrDefects5">Sample 5 defect count</label><input id="attrDefects5" min="0" step="1" type="number" value="2"/></td><td><label class="sr-only" for="attrOpportunity5">Sample 5 inspection opportunity</label><input id="attrOpportunity5" min="0.001" step="0.5" type="number" value="2"/></td><td id="attrRate5">—</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><label class="sr-only" for="attrDefects6">Sample 6 defect count</label><input id="attrDefects6" min="0" step="1" type="number" value="5"/></td><td><label class="sr-only" for="attrOpportunity6">Sample 6 inspection opportunity</label><input id="attrOpportunity6" min="0.001" step="0.5" type="number" value="2"/></td><td id="attrRate6">—</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><label class="sr-only" for="attrDefects7">Sample 7 defect count</label><input id="attrDefects7" min="0" step="1" type="number" value="4"/></td><td><label class="sr-only" for="attrOpportunity7">Sample 7 inspection opportunity</label><input id="attrOpportunity7" min="0.001" step="0.5" type="number" value="2"/></td><td id="attrRate7">—</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><label class="sr-only" for="attrDefects8">Sample 8 defect count</label><input id="attrDefects8" min="0" step="1" type="number" value="11"/></td><td><label class="sr-only" for="attrOpportunity8">Sample 8 inspection opportunity</label><input id="attrOpportunity8" min="0.001" step="0.5" type="number" value="2"/></td><td id="attrRate8">—</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
</div>
<div class="button-row"><button class="btn" id="analyzeAttributeBtn" type="button">Analyze and choose chart</button><button class="btn secondary" id="equalOpportunityBtn" type="button">Load equal opportunity</button><button class="btn secondary" id="unequalOpportunityBtn" type="button">Load unequal opportunity</button><button class="btn secondary" id="resetAttributeBtn" type="button">Reset lab</button></div>
<p aria-live="polite" class="status-line" id="attributeMessage"></p>
<div class="chart-wrap"><canvas aria-describedby="attributeDescription" aria-label="Selected c or u control chart" height="360" id="attributeCanvas" role="img" width="720"></canvas><p class="chart-caption" id="attributeDescription">Control chart with centre line and 3σ limits.</p></div>
<div class="legend-row"><span class="legend-key"><span class="legend-swatch"></span>Observed value</span><span class="legend-key"><span class="legend-swatch center"></span>Centre line</span><span class="legend-key"><span class="legend-swatch limit"></span>Control limits</span></div>
</div>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>Interpret cautiously:</strong> a point beyond a control limit signals special-cause investigation; it does not identify the root cause. Also verify independence, rational subgrouping, and a stable inspection method.</div>
</section>
